From: =?UTF-8?q?Lu=C3=ADs=20Duarte?= <lduarte@cloudflare.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 22:26:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Size a text field from its size attribute

A text input with no specified width measured 300px under a max-content
constraint, and as much of a definite space as it got up to that: an
auto-width field in an inline-block came out well over 100px wider than
in Chrome. HTML renders a text-entry field wide enough for `size`
characters, 20 by default, and Blink counts them as `size` average
character widths (the font's OS/2 average, rounded) plus one widest
character less the average (on Linux, the width of the font's `head`
bounding box), so the field's width now follows the font and the
attribute. A font without an OS/2 average counts in the advance of a
zero, as in Blink.

The field still shrinks into a narrower definite space, measured as the
content box the field is given rather than the box around its padding
and border, and its min-content width stays zero. Layout reads `size`
outside the style diff, so setting the attribute damages the field's
layout; a value past HTML's integer range falls back to the default.
The font is consulted only when the width is not otherwise known. The UA sheet does not shrink a field's
font to Chrome's 13.33px, so a field here is wider than Chrome's for the
same page, and a hard minimum would overflow containers that fit it in
Chrome. A number field's spin button is not counted.
